[step] Unavoidable core-clearance level boundary: the two measured +Z planes are the finite core receiver floor at Z−0.35 and the surrounding floor at Z0. The rounded core plan requires 0.25 mm running room plus 0.10 mm cavity-floor transition beneath the complete core footprint. This pick is outside that rounded plan. The same-normal plane comparison identifies the cavity perimeter; it does not identify material projecting into the required core room. Core, hardware and column reference poses remain fixed. The post-live report records this original-STL face comparison; it is not a whole-part thickness result.
alternatives: Extending the lowered floor through the surrounding platform would change unrelated hardware and column floor datums. A perimeter run-out could retain the complete 0.35 mm core room, but it would still join these two required levels. Preserve the named receiver floor and surrounding datums.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-90.733 y=465.372 z=0.000

[ceiling] Unavoidable flat lifting interface: these are the two flat handhold lifting ceilings at Z29.25, 35.25 mm above the standing underside, with the complete 12 mm roof section. Their flat finger bearing and the shared R6 end returns define the gripping surface. Tree(auto)/Default support can reach through the downward and outward handhold openings and the open seam before assembly. Actual support cleanup, bare-surface comfort and loaded lifting remain unqualified for these complete handholds.
alternatives: A sloped working ceiling changes the lifting bearing and finger opening. Filling beneath it blocks upward finger entry. Retain the flat roof and its open support-removal approaches.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-97.979 y=224.075 z=29.250
click: x=97.979 y=224.075 z=29.250

[ceiling] These Z102.15 faces are the lower ends of the two interleaved Y-seam screw-bearing blocks. The adjacent printed block faces retain their 0.50 mm running/support allowance. Each flat end preserves the defined bearing length around the printed screw passage. Tree(auto)/Default support is accessible from the empty seam and enclosure interior before the halves interleave. The raw ceiling finding does not establish physical removal effort.
alternatives: A corbel beneath an end enters the neighboring block’s assembly envelope. Sloping the end removes part of the defined screw-bearing length. A continuous post prevents interleaving. Keep the block end and its accessible support.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-97.625 y=209.975 z=102.150
click: x=97.625 y=209.975 z=102.150

[ceiling] These Z37.25 flats are the lower screws’ washer-bearing floors. Each 8 mm-deep U-pocket receives one flat M5 washer, retains the complete 3 mm annular backing and has a printed Ø5.6 screw passage. The nominal head recess is 2 mm. Support must leave through the inward-open pocket and handhold before installing the loose washer and screw. The current native stock evidence has its own identified inputs; this face lint does not qualify cleanup or washer-handling ease.
alternatives: Sloping a floor tilts the flat washer. Moving the floor changes screw seating, engagement and blind-tip room. Closing the inward access adds the removed cup strip and obstructs hardware placement. Retain the flat bearing and open access.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-97.200 y=208.900 z=37.250
click: x=97.200 y=208.900 z=37.250

[ceiling] Unavoidable square capture interface: these Z169.45 flats are the rear slide hooks’ catches. The mating foot remains at Z168.70, leaving 0.25 mm running room plus 0.25 mm for each of the two supported opposing faces. The square catch retains the top against lifting without introducing a ramp along that bearing. The west PRV opening divides its run. Tree(auto)/Default support leaves through the open slide channels before assembly; current-part removal effort remains unqualified.
alternatives: Sloping the catch introduces a camming bearing. Filling the channel beneath it blocks the mating foot’s slide. Retain the square retention profile and supported-face allowances.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
